# the simulation's types and its one piece of state
#
# In the C source the playsim is some three hundred file-scope variables
# spread over twenty files. Here it is a single Game record, passed to
# everything. A demo run is then a function of a WAD, a Game and a stream of
# commands, and nothing hides in a global.
#
# Two things the C code does with pointers are done differently:
#
# - Map structures (sectors, lines, subsectors) are referred to by number, as
#   level.mach stores them. Objects that live and die during play (map
#   objects, door and platform movers) are still pointers.
# - A thinker's function pointer is a kind byte. The C code compares that
#   pointer against known functions to ask "is this a map object?"; a kind
#   answers that directly, and the dispatch is one chain in tick.mach.
#
# Mach does not allow modules to import each other in a circle, and the C
# files call each other freely. The two calls that would close a circle go
# through function pointers held here: entering a state runs an action
# (enemy.mach and pspr.mach, far above world.mach), and crossing, using or
# shooting a line triggers a special (spec.mach).

use std.allocator;
use std.types.bool.bool;
use std.types.result.res;
use std.types.size.usize;
use std.types.string.str;

use doom.fixed.Fixed;
use doom.level;
use doom.random;
use doom.rdata;

